  "raw": "{\n  \"audits\": [\n    {\n      \"questionId\": \"q001\",\n      \"decision\": \"accept\",\n      \"assessedIds\": [\"cnm_p172\"],\n      \"supportIds\": [\"cnm_p159\"],\n      \"routes\": [],\n      \"derivedAnswer\": \"C: 420\",\n      \"mathematicalReason\": \"The least common denominator is lcm(2,3,4,5,6,7) = 2^2·3·5·7 = 420. Finding the least common denominator is explicitly within cnm_p172's common-denominator procedure, with least-common-multiple computation as support. Actually adding the fractions is not requested.\",\n      \"candidateFindings\": [],\n      \"needsNewNodes\": [],\n      \"limitations\": [],\n      \"figureEvidence\": []\n    },\n    {\n      \"questionId\": \"q002\",\n      \"decision\": \"accept\",\n      \"assessedIds\": [\"cnm_h200\", \"math:modular-congruence\"],\n      \"supportIds\": [],\n      \"routes\": [],\n      \"derivedAnswer\": \"B: 1999\",\n      \"mathematicalReason\": \"Applying the recurrence modulo 10 gives the block 4,7,1,8,9,7,6,3,9,2,1,3, whose sum is 60. The following two terms are again 4,7, so the recurrence repeats this block. Thus S_1992 = 166·60 = 9960. The next six terms sum to 36, while the next seven sum to 42, giving S_1998 = 9996 and S_1999 = 10002. Since all terms are nonnegative, 1999 is the first crossing. Both recurrence computation and reduction of sums modulo 10 are genuinely assessed by this route.\",\n      \"candidateFindings\": [],\n      \"needsNewNodes\": [],\n      \"limitations\": [],\n      \"figureEvidence\": []\n    },\n    {\n      \"questionId\": \"q003\",\n      \"decision\": \"unresolved\",\n      \"assessedIds\": [],\n      \"supportIds\": [],\n      \"routes\": [],\n      \"derivedAnswer\": \"D: If Q is true then P is false.\",\n      \"mathematicalReason\": \"The original statement is P ⇒ ¬Q. Its contrapositive is ¬(¬Q) ⇒ ¬P, or Q ⇒ ¬P. Both statements are false exactly when P and Q are simultaneously true.\",\n      \"candidateFindings\": [],\n      \"needsNewNodes\": [\n        \"If existing logic scopes do not cover it: equivalence of a conditional and its contrapositive, including conditionals with negated conclusions.\"\n      ],\n      \"limitations\": [\n        \"The answer is determined, but catalog coverage of contraposition remains unresolved.\",\n        \"Request scope lookup for cnm_h012 (Sufficient and Necessary Conditions), cnm_h013 (Necessary and Sufficient Conditions), and cnm_071 (Definitions, Propositions, and Theorems). Their titles alone neither establish nor exclude coverage, so a new-node requirement cannot yet be confirmed.\"\n      ],\n      \"figureEvidence\": []\n    },\n    {\n      \"questionId\": \"q004\",\n      \"decision\": \"unresolved\",\n      \"assessedIds\": [],\n      \"supportIds\": [\"cnm_008\", \"cnm_042\", \"cnm_197\", \"cnm_h031\"],\n      \"routes\": [],\n      \"derivedAnswer\": \"C: 10\",\n      \"mathematicalReason\": \"Substitution into the downward-opening graph gives |8-a| - |2-a| = 2. For a ≤ 2 this difference is 6, and for a ≥ 8 it is -6; hence 2 < a < 8, where 10-2a = 2 gives a = 4. For the upward-opening graph, substitution gives |8-c| - |2-c| = -2. The same branch analysis gives 10-2c = -2, so c = 6. Therefore a+c = 10; the corresponding vertical parameters are b = 7 and d = 1. The proposed support scopes are relevant to simplification, linear solving, and a graphical or piecewise route, but their supplied definitions do not explicitly cover the central parameter-recovery skill.\",\n      \"candidateFindings\": [],\n      \"needsNewNodes\": [\n        \"Determining parameters of translated absolute-value graphs y = ±|x-h|+k from prescribed points, including justification of the applicable branches.\"\n      ],\n      \"limitations\": [\n        \"The mathematical answer is determined. A directly matching assessed scope for translated absolute-value graphs and their parameter constraints is not established by the supplied definitions.\"\n      ],\n      \"figureEvidence\": []\n 